# MyCloud

A self-hosted personal cloud file manager that runs on your computer with Docker and stores files in your own Syncthing folder.

MyCloud gives you a browser interface to view, upload, download, and convert files while Syncthing handles syncing them to your other trusted devices.

## Features

- View files from a local Syncthing folder
- Upload files and folders from a browser
- Download files from a browser
- Convert PDF files to DOCX
- Convert JPG, PNG, and WebP images
- Convert supported audio and video formats
- Protect the website with a password
- Run locally with Docker Desktop
- Sync files between your devices using Syncthing
- Optional secure remote access using Tailscale
- No router port forwarding required

## How It Works

```text
Browser
  |
  v
MyCloud web app in Docker
  |
  v
Mounted Syncthing folder on the host computer
  |
  v
Syncthing
  |
  +-- Phone
  +-- Laptop
  +-- Raspberry Pi
  +-- Other trusted devices
```

## Requirements

Install these on the Windows computer that will host MyCloud:

- Docker Desktop
- Syncthing
- Git
- Optional: Tailscale for secure access away from home

## Setup

### 1. Clone the repository

```powershell
git clone https://github.com/vaishantn/iCloud_alternative.git
cd iCloud_alternative
```

### 2. Set up Syncthing

1. Install and open Syncthing.
2. Create or choose the Syncthing folder that MyCloud should manage.
3. Open the Syncthing interface in your browser:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8384
```

4. In Syncthing, go to:

```text
Actions -> Settings -> GUI
```

5. Copy the Syncthing API key.
6. Copy the Windows path of the Syncthing folder.

Example:

```text
C:\Users\YourWindowsUser\Syncthing_save_folder
```

### 3. Create your private configuration

Copy the example file:

```powershell
Copy-Item .env_example .env
```

Open it:

```powershell
notepad .env
```

Fill in your own values:

```env
FLASK_SECRET_KEY=replace-with-a-long-random-secret
APP_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-strong-password
SYNCTHING_API_KEY=replace-with-your-syncthing-api-key

SYNCTHING_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8384

# Leave this unchanged.
SYNCED_FOLDER_PATH=/data/syncthing

# Change this to your own Syncthing folder.
# Use forward slashes on Windows.
HOST_SYNCED_FOLDER_PATH=C:/Users/YourWindowsUser/Syncthing_save_folder

APP_PORT=5000
DEMO_MODE=false
```

Generate a secure Flask secret key:

```powershell
python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
```

Copy the generated value into:

```env
FLASK_SECRET_KEY=your-generated-value
```

Do not commit `.env`. It contains your password, secret key, API key, and local folder path.

### 4. Start MyCloud

Make sure Docker Desktop is running.

From the project folder, run:

```powershell
docker compose up -d --build
```

Open MyCloud:

```text
http://localhost:5000
```

Log in with the password you set for `APP_PASSWORD`.

### 5. Test MyCloud

1. Confirm the dashboard shows files in your Syncthing folder.
2. Upload a small test file.
3. Confirm it appears in your Windows Syncthing folder.
4. Confirm it syncs to other devices connected through Syncthing.

## Daily Commands

### Start MyCloud

```powershell
docker compose up -d
```

### Stop MyCloud

```powershell
docker compose down
```

### Rebuild after code changes

```powershell
docker compose up -d --build
```

### Check container status

```powershell
docker compose ps
```

### View logs

```powershell
docker compose logs --tail=100
```

## Access Away From Home

MyCloud normally runs only on the computer hosting Docker:

```text
http://localhost:5000
```

To use MyCloud from a phone, another laptop, school, or another network, use Tailscale.

Tailscale creates a private encrypted network between devices you authorize. You do not need to open router ports or expose MyCloud publicly to the internet.

### 1. Install Tailscale

Install Tailscale on:

- The Windows computer running Docker and MyCloud
- Every phone, tablet, laptop, or other device that should access MyCloud

Download Tailscale:

```text
[https://tailscale.com/download](https://tailscale.com/download)
```

On Windows:

1. Download and install Tailscale.
2. Open Tailscale from the system tray.
3. Select **Log in**.
4. Sign in to your Tailscale account.

On your phone or another computer:

1. Install the Tailscale app.
2. Sign in with the same Tailscale account.

### 2. Connect the host computer

On the computer running MyCloud, open PowerShell and run:

```powershell
tailscale up
```

Complete the sign-in flow in your browser, then check:

```powershell
tailscale status
```

### 3. Share MyCloud over Tailscale

Make sure MyCloud is running first:

```powershell
docker compose up -d
```

Then run:

```powershell
tailscale serve localhost:5000
```

Check the Tailscale Serve configuration:

```powershell
tailscale serve status
```

Tailscale will provide an HTTPS URL similar to:

```text
[https://your-computer-name.your-tailnet.ts.net](https://your-computer-name.your-tailnet.ts.net)
```

Open that URL from another device that is signed in to your Tailscale network.

### Stop Tailscale access

```powershell
tailscale serve off
```

### Tailscale Troubleshooting

#### `tailscale serve status` says `No serve config`

Run:

```powershell
tailscale serve localhost:5000
tailscale serve status
```

#### The Tailscale HTTPS URL does not load

Check that:

- MyCloud is still running:

```powershell
docker compose ps
```

- Tailscale is connected on the computer hosting MyCloud:

```powershell
tailscale status
```

- Tailscale is installed and connected on the remote device.
- The remote device is signed in to the same authorized Tailscale account.
- `tailscale serve status` points to `localhost:5000`.

## Supported Conversions

| Source | Target | Docker support |
|---|---|---|
| PDF | DOCX | Supported |
| JPG, JPEG, PNG, WebP | JPG, PNG, WebP | Supported |
| MP3, WAV, MP4, MKV, MOV, AVI, M4A | MP3, WAV, MP4, MKV | Supported when FFmpeg supports the input |
| DOCX | PDF | Not supported in Docker |

## Limitations

- DOCX-to-PDF conversion requires Microsoft Word and Windows-specific automation, so it is not available inside the Linux Docker container.
- Syncthing must be installed and configured separately.
- MyCloud must be running for the website to be available.
- MyCloud is designed for personal or trusted-device use, not public internet hosting.
- This setup is documented for Windows with Docker Desktop.

## Security

- Do not commit `.env`.
- Use a strong `APP_PASSWORD`.
- Keep your Syncthing API key private.
- Do not forward port `5000` on your router.
- Use Tailscale for secure remote access instead of exposing the app publicly.
- Install Tailscale only on devices you trust.

